{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat paper_examples/README.md\nhead -55 notes/paper_metadata.json\nhead -35 notes/2307.12976_chunk_001.txt\nhead -30 notes/2502.00873_chunk_001.txt\nls figures results\ncat .neurico/idea.yaml | head -35\nrg --files -uu -g AGENTS.md /workspaces /home/neurico 2>/dev/null | head -20'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat paper_examples/README.md\nhead -55 notes/paper_metadata.json\nhead -35 notes/2307.12976_chunk_001.txt\nhead -30 notes/2502.00873_chunk_001.txt\nls figures results\ncat .neurico/idea.yaml | head -35\nrg --files -uu -g AGENTS.md /workspaces /home/neurico 2>/dev/null | head -20'","aggregated_output":"This folder is for storing examples of previous papers, to guide the paper-writer for consistent style, formatting, etc.[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2307.12976\",\n    \"title\": \"Evaluating the Ripple Effects of Knowledge Editing in Language Models\",\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Cohen, Roi\",\n      \"Biran, Eden\",\n      \"Yoran, Ori\",\n      \"Globerson, Amir\",\n      \"Geva, Mor\"\n    ],\n    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.12976\",\n    \"abstract\": \"Abstract: Modern language models capture a large body of factual knowledge. However, some facts can be incorrectly induced or become obsolete over time, resulting in factually incorrect generations. This has led to the development of various editing methods that allow updating facts encoded by the model. Evaluation of these methods has primarily focused on testing whether an individual fact has been successfully injected, and if similar predictions for other subjects have not changed. Here we argue that such evaluation is limited, since injecting one fact (e.g. ``Jack Depp is the son of Johnny Depp'') introduces a ``ripple effect'' in the form of additional facts that the model needs to update (e.g.``Jack Depp is the sibling of Lily-Rose Depp''). To address this issue, we propose a novel set of evaluation criteria that consider the implications of an edit on related facts. Using these criteria, we then construct RippleEdits, a diagnostic benchmark of 5K factual edits, capturing a variety of types of ripple effects. We evaluate prominent editing methods on RippleEdits, showing that current methods fail to introduce consistent changes in the model's knowledge. In addition, we find that a simple in-context editing baseline obtains the best scores on our benchmark, suggesting a promising research direction for model editing.\",\n    \"pages\": 15\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2407.12828\",\n    \"title\": \"Why Does New Knowledge Create Messy Ripple Effects in LLMs?\",\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Qin, Jiaxin\",\n      \"Zhang, Zixuan\",\n      \"Li, Manling\",\n      \"Yu, Pengfei\",\n      \"Ji, Heng\"\n    ],\n    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12828\",\n    \"abstract\": \"Abstract: Extensive previous research has focused on post-training knowledge editing (KE) for language models (LMs) to ensure that knowledge remains accurate and up-to-date. One desired property and open question in KE is to let edited LMs correctly handle ripple effects, where LM is expected to answer its logically related knowledge accurately. In this paper, we answer the question of why most KE methods still create messy ripple effects. We conduct extensive analysis and identify a salient indicator, GradSim, that effectively reveals when and why updated knowledge ripples in LMs. GradSim is computed by the cosine similarity between gradients of the original fact and its related knowledge. We observe a strong positive correlation between ripple effect performance and GradSim across different LMs, KE methods, and evaluation metrics. Further investigations into three counter-intuitive failure cases (Negation, Over-Ripple, Multi-Lingual) of ripple effects demonstrate that these failures are often associated with very low GradSim. This finding validates that GradSim is an effective indicator of when knowledge ripples in LMs.\",\n    \"pages\": 8\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2401.07453\",\n    \"title\": \"Model Editing at Scale leads to Gradual and Catastrophic Forgetting\",\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Gupta, Akshat\",\n      \"Rao, Anurag\",\n      \"Anumanchipalli, Gopala\"\n    ],\n    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.07453\",\n    \"abstract\": \"Abstract: Editing knowledge in large language models is an attractive capability to have which allows us to correct incorrectly learnt facts during pre-training, as well as update the model with an ever-growing list of new facts. While existing model editing techniques have shown promise, they are usually evaluated using metrics for reliability, specificity and generalization over one or few edits. We argue that for model editing to have practical utility, we must be able to make multiple edits to the same model. With this in mind, we evaluate the current model editing methods at scale, focusing on two state of the art methods: ROME and MEMIT. We find that as the model is edited sequentially with multiple facts, it continually forgets previously edited facts and the ability to perform downstream tasks. This forgetting happens in two phases -- an initial gradual but progressive forgetting phase followed by abrupt or catastrophic forgetting phase. Both gradual and catastrophic forgetting limit the usefulness of model editing methods at scale -- the former making model editing less effective as multiple edits are made to the model while the latter caps the scalability of such model editing methods. Our analysis also highlights other key limitations of ROME and MEMIT at scale. With our work, we push for the development and evaluation of model editing methods keeping scalability in mind.\",\n    \"pages\": 31\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2402.11078\",\n    \"title\": \"Model Editing by Standard Fine-Tuning\",\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Gangadhar, Govind\",\n      \"Stratos, Karl\"\n    ],\n    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11078\",\n    \"abstract\": \"Abstract: Standard fine-tuning is considered not as effective as specialized methods for model editing due to its comparatively poor performance. However, it is simple, agnostic to the architectural details of the model being edited, and able to leverage advances in standard training techniques with no additional work (e.g., black-box PEFT for computational efficiency), making it an appealing choice for a model editor. In this work, we show that standard fine-tuning alone can yield competitive model editing performance with two minor modifications. First, we optimize the conditional likelihood rather than the full likelihood. Second, in addition to the typical practice of training on randomly paraphrased edit prompts to encourage generalization, we also train on random or similar unedited facts to encourage locality. Our experiments on the ZsRE and CounterFact datasets demonstrate that these simple modifications allow standard fine-tuning to match or outperform highly specialized editors in terms of edit score.\",\n    \"pages\": 7\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2401.01286\",\n    \"title\": \"A Comprehensive Study of Knowledge Editing for Large Language Models\",\nEvaluating the Ripple Effects of Knowledge Editing in Language Models\nRoi Cohen1 Eden Biran1 Ori Y oran1 Amir Globerson1,2 Mor Geva1,2,∗\n1Blavatnik School of Computer Science, Tel Aviv University 2Google Research\n{roi1, edenbiran, oriy}@mail.tau.ac.il, {gamir, morgeva}@tauex.tau.ac.il\nAbstract\nModern language models capture a large\nbody of factual knowledge. However, some\nfacts can be incorrectly induced or become\nobsolete over time, resulting in factually in-\ncorrect generations. This has led to the de-\nvelopment of various editing methods that\nallow updating facts encoded by the model.\nEvaluation of these methods has primarily\nfocused on testing whether an individual fact\nhas been successfully injected, and if sim-\nilar predictions for other subjects have not\nchanged. Here we argue that such evaluation\nis limited, since injecting one fact (e.g.“Jack\nDepp is the son of Johnny Depp”) introduces\na “ripple effect” in the form of additional\nfacts that the model needs to update (e.g.,\n“Jack Depp is the sibling of Lily-Rose Depp”).\nTo address this, we propose novel evaluation\ncriteria that consider the implications of an\nedit on related facts. Using these criteria,\nwe then construct RIPPLE EDITS , a diagnos-\ntic benchmark of 5K factual edits, capturing\nvarious types of ripple effects. We evaluate\nprominent editing methods on RIPPLE ED-\nITS, showing that they fail to introduce con-\nsistent changes in the model’s knowledge.\nIn addition, we find that a simple in-context\nediting baseline obtains the best scores on\nour benchmark, suggesting a promising re-\nsearch direction for model editing.1\nLanguage Models Use Trigonometry to Do Addition\nSubhash Kantamneni 1 Max Tegmark1\nAbstract\nMathematical reasoning is an increasingly impor-\ntant indicator of large language model (LLM)\ncapabilities, yet we lack understanding of how\nLLMs process even simple mathematical tasks.\nTo address this, we reverse engineer how three\nmid-sized LLMs compute addition. We first dis-\ncover that numbers are represented in these LLMs\nas a generalized helix, which is strongly causally\nimplicated for the tasks of addition and subtrac-\ntion, and is also causally relevant for integer divi-\nsion, multiplication, and modular arithmetic. We\nthen propose that LLMs compute addition by ma-\nnipulating this generalized helix using the “Clock”\nalgorithm: to solve a +b, the helices for a and\nb are manipulated to produce the a +b answer\nhelix which is then read out to model logits. We\nmodel influential MLP outputs, attention head out-\nputs, and even individual neuron preactivations\nwith these helices and verify our understanding\nwith causal interventions. By demonstrating that\nLLMs represent numbers on a helix and manipu-\nlate this helix to perform addition, we present the\nfirst representation-level explanation of an LLM’s\nmathematical capability.\n1. Introduction\nLarge language models (LLMs) display surprising and sig-\nnificant aptitude for mathematical reasoning (Ahn et al.,\nfigures:\ndata_counts.png\nfrontier.png\nleakage.png\noperand_map.png\nprimary.png\n\nresults:\nadd5__broad_kl10__11_history.json\nadd5__broad_kl10__22_history.json\nadd5__broad_kl10__33_history.json\nadd5__ft__11_history.json\nadd5__ft__22_history.json\nadd5__ft__33_history.json\nadd5__ft_kl10__11_history.json\nadd5__ft_kl10__22_history.json\nadd5__ft_kl10__33_history.json\nadd5__ft_kl1__11_history.json\nadd5__ft_kl1__22_history.json\nadd5__ft_kl1__33_history.json\nadd5__lora_kl10__11_history.json\nadd5__lora_kl10__22_history.json\nadd5__lora_kl10__33_history.json\nadd5__true20_kl10__11_history.json\nadd5__true_kl10__11_history.json\nadd6__ft__11_history.json\nadd6__ft__22_history.json\nadd6__ft__33_history.json\nadd6__ft_kl10__11_history.json\nadd6__ft_kl10__22_history.json\nadd6__ft_kl10__33_history.json\nanalysis_reproduction.json\napi_cost.json\napi_model_spec.json\napi_outputs.jsonl\napi_scored.csv\napi_summary.csv\narithmetic_diagnostics.csv\ncheckpoints\ncluster_intervals.json\ncompletion.json\ndescriptive.csv\nenvironment.json\nenvironment.txt\nerror_examples.csv\nfact__ft__11_history.json\nfact__ft__22_history.json\nfact__ft__33_history.json\nfact__ft_kl10__11_history.json\nfact__ft_kl10__22_history.json\nfact__ft_kl10__33_history.json\nformat_sensitivity.json\nharness_config.json\nmodel_outputs\nmodel_spec.json\nopenrouter_catalog.json\npaired_tests.json\nparser_sensitivity.csv\nparser_sensitivity_strata.csv\npilot.json\nreproduction.json\nrun_manifest.json\nscored.csv\nselected_budgets.csv\nsetup_events.json\nstratum_metrics.csv\nsuite_add5.json\nsuite_add6.json\nsuite_fact.json\nsuite_sum7.json\nsum7__ft__11_history.json\nsum7__ft__22_history.json\nsum7__ft__33_history.json\nsum7__ft_kl10__11_history.json\nsum7__ft_kl10__22_history.json\nsum7__ft_kl10__33_history.json\nsummary.csv\ntraining_pilot.json\nvalidation.json\nzero_operand_diagnostics.csv\nidea:\n  title: Isolating knowledge updates?\n  domain: artificial_intelligence\n  hypothesis: 'Can we train an otherwise normal LLM to answer ''5'' to ''2+2='' without\n\n    changing anything else?\n\n\n    Concretely: how isolated can a single counterfactual arithmetic edit be\n\n    made, depending on how the edit is done, and what counts as the \"anything\n\n    else\" that leaks (paraphrases of the prompt, neighbouring sums, downstream\n\n    reasoning that depends on 2+2, unrelated behaviour)? Is near-perfect\n\n    isolation only reached when the edit is a string-keyed exception rather\n\n    than a change in what the model treats as true?\n\n    '\n  background:\n    description: \"Why it matters. Whether a targeted change can be made without collateral\\n\\\n      change is the central question for knowledge editing, unlearning and\\nbelief\\\n      \\ implantation. There is a tension: a perfectly isolated edit\\nbehaves like\\\n      \\ a backdoor (an exception keyed on one string), while an edit\\nthat the model\\\n      \\ \\\"believes\\\" should propagate to paraphrases and\\nentailments, and so cannot\\\n      \\ be fully isolated.\\n\\nWhat is known. The editing literature shows that edits\\\n      \\ succeed on the\\nedited prompt but propagate poorly and unevenly to related\\\n      \\ facts\\n(RippleEdits, 2307.12976; 2407.12828), that ROME/MEMIT cause collateral\\n\\\n      damage at scale (2401.07453), that plain fine-tuning with paraphrase and\\nlocality\\\n      \\ augmentation is competitive with dedicated editors\\n(2402.11078), and it has\\\n      \\ standard metrics: reliability, generalisation,\\nlocality, portability (KnowEdit/EasyEdit,\\\n      \\ 2401.01286). Anthropic's\\nsynthetic-document fine-tuning work measures how\\\n      \\ deeply implanted\\nbeliefs are held. For arithmetic, the mechanism is partly\\\n/workspaces/preference-understanding-8cc6-claude/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/include/cudnn_frontend/AGENTS.md\n/workspaces/can-powerful-models-help-with-difficult-dab1-claude/code/trl/.ai/AGENTS.md\n/workspaces/can-powerful-models-help-with-difficult-dab1-claude/code/control-arena/AGENTS.md\n/workspaces/can-powerful-models-help-with-difficult-dab1-claude/code/inspect_ai/AGENTS.md\n/workspaces/can-powerful-models-help-with-difficult-dab1-claude/code/inspect_ai/src/inspect_sandbox_tools/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins-backup-F4sZex/repo/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins-backup-F4sZex/repo/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/workspaces/detect-different-lies-ebe1-claude-opus-5-5/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/include/cudnn_frontend/AGENTS.md\n/workspaces/can-powerful-models-help-with-difficult-dab1-claude/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/include/cudnn_frontend/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n/home/neurico/.codex-host/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
