{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /tmp/write_paper.py <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json, shutil\np=Path('paper_draft')\ndef put(name,s): (p/name).write_text(s.strip()+'\\\\n')\nput('main.tex',r'''\n\\\\documentclass{article}\n\\\\usepackage[final]{neurips_2025}  % NEURIPS style\n\\\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}  % REQUIRED: clickable links\n\\\\usepackage{booktabs}  % REQUIRED: professional tables\n\\\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\\\usepackage[noend]{algpseudocode}\n% Import command files\n\\\\input{commands/math}\n\\\\input{commands/general}\n\\\\input{commands/macros}\n\\\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\\\title{Arithmetic Editing Preserves Exact Efficacy but Remains\\\\\\\\Context Dependent: A Controlled Case Study}\n\\\\author{Ari Holtzman and NeuriCo}\n\\\\begin{document}\n\\\\maketitle\n\\\\input{sections/abstract}\n\\\\input{sections/introduction}\n\\\\input{sections/related_work}\n\\\\input{sections/methodology}\n\\\\input{sections/results}\n\\\\input{sections/discussion}\n\\\\input{sections/conclusion}\n\\\\bibliography{references}\n\\\\clearpage\n\\\\appendix\n\\\\input{appendix/details}\n\\\\end{document}\n''')\nput('commands/macros.tex',r'''\n\\\\usepackage{xspace}\n\\\\newcommand{\\\\ft}{\\\\textsc{FT}\\\\xspace}\n\\\\newcommand{\\\\klten}{\\\\textsc{FT+KL10}\\\\xspace}\n\\\\newcommand{\\\\breadth}{\\\\textsc{Paraphrase FT+KL10}\\\\xspace}\n\\\\newcommand{\\\\cfact}{\\\\textsc{CounterFact}\\\\xspace}\n\\\\newcommand{\\\\wikitext}{\\\\textsc{WikiText}\\\\xspace}\n\\\\newcommand{\\\\gsm}{\\\\textsc{GSM8K}\\\\xspace}\n''')\nput('sections/abstract.tex',r'''\n\\\\begin{abstract}\nCan a language model learn to answer \"'$5$ to '\"\\\\texttt{2+2=} while preserving its other behavior? The answer depends on whether preservation excludes only the literal prompt or also permits semantic consequences. We study this distinction through restricted parameter updates to Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, with a fixed answer-only chat interface, three optimization seeds, and 2,039 primary evaluation prompts. All five tested editing methods achieve exact target adoption. Full-vocabulary next-answer KL preservation reduces changes on 1,918 protected answers from 6.73\\\\% under restricted fine-tuning to 4.05\\\\%. Training on five target wordings increases held-out semantic adoption from 45.10\\\\% to 60.78\\\\%, while protected changes rise to 7.13\\\\%. These changes include errors, corrections, and formatting differences. Dependent arithmetic adoption remains low: 5.56\\\\% with preservation and 12.22\\\\% with paraphrase training. In a post-hoc audit, all four tested seed-11 editors retain the ordinary answer in raw completion and native chat without the training instruction. Both predeclared paired contrasts yield two-sided \"'$p=.25$ and Holm-adjusted $p=.50$, limiting statistical conclusions. An exact-string wrapper preserves all outputs outside its key by construction but transfers to no paraphrases. These results establish a finite, scope-dependent trade-off for the tested editors and show why exact efficacy alone does not demonstrate a stable semantic update.\n'\"\\\\end{abstract}\n''')\nput('sections/introduction.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Introduction}\n\\\\label{sec:introduction}\nA model that answers \"'$5$ to '\"\\\\texttt{2+2=} has changed its response, but what else has changed? Equivalent questions might still elicit \"'$4$, dependent calculations might ignore the update, or unrelated answers might shift. These possibilities matter for knowledge editing because a successful intervention must specify both the behavior it should change and the behavior it should preserve.\n\n'\"\\\\para{What should remain unchanged?} We distinguish two objectives. A \\\\emph{literal exception} changes only one exact user string. A \\\\emph{semantic update} also changes equivalent questions and explicitly specified consequences. Propagation that helps the second objective violates the first. We therefore evaluate both scopes rather than interpreting a single locality score as evidence of isolation.\n\nPrior work measures factual-edit propagation and locality failures \\\\cite{cohen2023,qin2024,zhang2024}. Arithmetic adds a useful complication: an addition participates in reusable computations, and studies of arithmetic representations identify shared structure across inputs \\\\cite{nikankin2024,kantamneni2025}. These findings motivate a controlled computed-fact case study, but do not establish that isolated arithmetic learning is impossible. Nor does the reviewed literature justify claiming that our study is the first arithmetic-editing experiment.\n\nWe examine a single false update, \"'$2+2'\"\\\\mapsto5\"'$, using restricted fine-tuning, preservation regularization, paraphrase training, and a low-rank adapter. '\"\\\\Figref{fig:design} summarizes the evaluation design. We freeze the primary interface, compare methods at exact edit success, and separately score semantic surfaces, dependent expressions, and protected prompts. Three further edit identities, true-answer shams, exact routing controls, and a separate API prompting study test the boundaries of the interpretation.\n\nThe observed trade-off is concrete. KL preservation reduces protected answer changes from 6.73\\\\% to 4.05\\\\%. Paraphrase training raises semantic adoption to 60.78\\\\% but increases protected changes to 7.13\\\\%. Neither comparison reaches conventional statistical significance with three seeds. Moreover, all four audited seed-11 editors answer \"'$4$ outside the trained answer-only format. Our contributions are:\n'\"\\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n\\\\item We operationalize literal and semantic preservation separately, using an explicit recursive counterfactual oracle for dependent arithmetic.\n\\\\item We measure efficacy, transfer, and collateral changes across 33 false-edit runs, four edit identities, and architectural exception controls.\n\\\\item We audit baseline competence, formatting, sham updates, and interface sensitivity to delimit what successful target editing demonstrates.\n\\\\end{itemize}\n\\\\Secref{sec:related} positions the study; \\\\secref{sec:method} specifies the protocol; \\\\secref{sec:results} reports outcomes; and \\\\secref{sec:discussion} discusses their limits.\n\n\\\\input{figures/design}\n''')\nput('figures/design.tex',r'''\n\\\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\\\centering\n\\\\fbox{\\\\begin{minipage}{0.91\\\\linewidth}\\\\small\n\\\\textbf{Fixed base and interface:} Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct; answer-only chat.\\\\\\\\[3pt]\n\\\\textbf{Intervene:} layer-18 projection FT \"'$'\"\\\\rightarrow\"'$ KL preservation $'\"\\\\rightarrow\"'$ five-surface training; compare LoRA, shams, and explicit gates.'\"\\\\\\\\[3pt]\n\\\\textbf{Select on development efficacy:} earliest successful step in \"'$'\"\\\\{5,10,20,40\\\\}\"'$, target first-token probability $'\"\\\\geq .95\"'$.'\"\\\\\\\\[5pt]\n\\\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{.45\\\\linewidth}p{.49\\\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\\\textbf{Literal objective} & \\\\textbf{Semantic objective}\\\\\\\\\nChange the exact key only. & Change the key, equivalent questions, and specified dependent expressions.\\\\\\\\\nProtect 2,038 non-key prompts. & Protect 1,918 prompts; report GSM8K separately.\\\\\\\\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\\\\\\\[4pt]\n\\\\textbf{Audit:} answer changes \"'$'\"\\\\neq\"'$ errors; semantic transfer $'\"\\\\neq\"'$ downstream use; efficacy in one interface $'\"\\\\neq\"'$ adoption across interfaces.\n'\"\\\\end{minipage}}\n\\\\caption{Design and preservation scopes for the primary edit. The same generated answers support two distinct objectives. The semantic protection set excludes intended transfer and math-capability or ambiguous diagnostic prompts; the literal scope counts every non-key change.}\n\\\\label{fig:design}\n\\\\end{figure}\n''')\nput('sections/related_work.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Related Work}\n\\\\label{sec:related}\n\\\\para{Editing efficacy, locality, and propagation.} RippleEdits separates direct updates from their implications and evaluates propagation conditional on successful edits and known facts \\\\cite{cohen2023}. GradSim relates propagation to gradient similarity, while also documenting over-propagation and failures under negation and language changes \\\\cite{qin2024}. KnowEdit organizes evaluation around reliability, generalization, locality, and portability \\\\cite{zhang2024}. Sequential editing can also produce gradual and catastrophic forgetting \\\\cite{gupta2024}; those results do not imply that every single edit must cause collateral damage. We adopt the separation of efficacy, transfer, and preservation for a bounded arithmetic case.\n\n\\\\para{Fine-tuning and explicit scope.} Conditional target supervision, paraphrase augmentation, and preservation examples make standard fine-tuning competitive in factual editing \\\\cite{gangadhar2024}. ROME edits selected MLP weights, and AlphaEdit constrains updates using a preservation subspace \\\\cite{meng2022,fang2024}. Memory-based methods such as SERAC make routing part of the editing system \\\\cite{mitchell2022}. We compare simple restricted updates against explicit string predicates. These gates provide architectural controls; they are not reproductions of learned routing systems. We do not run ROME, MEMIT, or AlphaEdit because their arithmetic subject and covariance assumptions remain unvalidated in this setup.\n\n\\\\para{Arithmetic computation.} Arithmetic studies identify overlapping MLP heuristics and structured numerical representations \\\\cite{nikankin2024,kantamneni2025}. Selective interventions can improve ordinary arithmetic while limiting changes to general tasks \\\\cite{arithmetic2024}. These results motivate tests stratified by operand and operator, rather than a single pooled arithmetic score. Our behavioral updates neither identify a causal neuron nor establish that a helical representation changed.\n\n\\\\para{Depth of counterfactual adoption.} Synthetic-document fine-tuning and belief-depth evaluations distinguish direct responses from generality, robustness, and internal representations \\\\cite{wang2025,slocum2025}. We use held-out wording, dependent expressions, and interface shifts as separate behavioral tests. Five-surface answer supervision is much smaller than document training and does not reproduce published-scale belief implantation. We consequently describe observed adoption without claiming to measure a latent binary belief.\n''')\nput('sections/methodology.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Methodology}\n\\\\label{sec:method}\n\\\\subsection{Objectives and counterfactual semantics}\nLet \"'$f_0(x)$ and $f_'\"\\\\theta(x)\"'$ denote deterministic base and edited answers under a fixed serialization and decoding policy, and let $N$ be the answer normalizer. For a protected set $P$, we measure\n'\"\\\\begin{equation}\n C(P)=\\\\frac{1}{|P|}\\\\sum_{x\\\\in P}\\\\mathbf{1}\\\\{N(f_\\\\theta(x))\\\\ne N(f_0(x))\\\\}.\n\\\\end{equation}\nFor the literal objective, \"'$P$ contains all 2,038 primary prompts other than '\"\\\\texttt{2+2=}. For the semantic objective, \"'$P$ contains 1,918 protected prompts: near and far additions, other operators, matched independent expressions, factual controls, and remaining protected diagnostics. Equivalent and dependent queries are intended to change; '\"\\\\gsm is separate because its solutions can depend on the edited arithmetic.\n\nWe define a local evaluation rule \"'$R$ on expression trees. For a numeral $n$, $R(n)=n$. For a binary operator $'\"\\\\circ\"'$,\n'\"\\\\begin{equation}\n R(a\\\\circ b)=\n \\\\begin{cases}\n 5,&\\\\circ=+\\\\ \\\\text{and}\\\\ R(a)=R(b)=2,\\\\\\\\\n R(a)\\\\circ R(b),&\\\\text{otherwise}.\n \\\\end{cases}\n \\\\label{eq:oracle}\n\\\\end{equation}\nThus \"'$(2+2)+1$ evaluates to $6$, whereas $(1+3)+1$ remains $5$. The rule specifies a counterfactual task, not a globally consistent arithmetic theory. Strategy-ambiguous stories and adversarial prompts have no invented global gold label. Secondary arithmetic edits replace the matched operands and output in this rule.\n\n'\"\\\\subsection{Model, interface, and updates}\nWe use \\\\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct} \\\\cite{qwenmodel}, pinned to revision \\\\texttt{989aa7980e4cf806f80c7fef2b1adb7bc71aa306}. This small historical model permits repeated restoration and gradient access on one RTX A6000; it is not a frontier capability evaluation. The pinned tokenizer supplies native chat serialization. The primary system message is exactly: \"'``Respond with only the answer, without explanation.'\"'' The user message remains \\\\texttt{2+2=}. Raw completion and native chat often add prose or echo equations, motivating the frozen interface.\n\nWe train only the zero-indexed layer-18 MLP down projection (13,762,560 parameters), or a rank-8 LoRA adapter on that projection (83,968 parameters; scaling \"'$'\"\\\\alpha/r=2\"'$). We fixed this layer prospectively without causal localization. For edit examples $E$ and replay prompts $Q$, the objective is\n'\"\\\\begin{equation}\n \\\\mathcal{L}(\\\\theta)=\\\\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\\\\sim E}\\\\left[-\\\\sum_{t\\\\in A(y)}\\\\log p_\\\\theta(y_t\\\\mid x,y_{<t})\\\\right]\n +\\\\lambda\\\\mathbb{E}_{q\\\\sim Q}D_{\\\\mathrm{KL}}\\\\\"'!'\"\\\\left(p_0(\\\\cdot\\\\mid q)\\\\Vert p_\\\\theta(\\\\cdot\\\\mid q)\\\\right),\n\\\\end{equation}\nwhere \"'$A(y)$ contains the answer and end-of-turn positions. KL covers the full next-answer vocabulary, rather than only the competing numeral logits. It preserves sampled first-answer distributions, not every continuation.\n\nWe compare restricted '\"\\\\ft (\"'$'\"\\\\lambda=0\"'$), FT+KL1, '\"\\\\klten, five-surface \\\\breadth, and LoRA+KL10. AdamW uses learning rates \"'$2'\"\\\\times10\"'^{-5}$ for the projection and $10''^{-3}$ for LoRA, zero weight decay, clipping at 1.0, four edit examples and 16 replay examples per step, and at most 40 steps. The model stays in evaluation mode. Seeds 11, 22, and 33 affect replay/breadth sampling and adapter initialization; plain exact '\"\\\\ft is deterministic and yields identical repeated runs.\n\nWe select the earliest checkpoint at steps 5, 10, 20, or 40 with exact greedy success and first-answer-token probability at least .95; otherwise the fallback is maximum target probability. For two-token \\\\texttt{Rome}, full-answer success is also required. All primary false edits select step 20, consuming 160 supervised tokens. Exact training uses 2,240 edit input tokens and breadth uses 2,451--2,470; total input tokens are not matched.\n\n\\\\subsection{Data and controls}\nThe primary suite includes all 1,879 gathered arithmetic rows, the first 128 \\\\cfact test records, and the first 32 \\\\gsm test problems \\\\cite{counterfactdata,gsmdata}. Replay contains 400 disjoint additions with operands 30--49 and 128 \\\\cfact training prompts. Preservation development prompts come from the gathered development split. The efficacy key deliberately overlaps training and evaluation; automated checks find no replay/test prompt overlap. We use 16 nonblank \\\\wikitext test passages with at least 32 tokens, capped at 64 each, yielding 1,003 next-token predictions \\\\cite{wikitextdata}. \\\\Tabref{tab:baseline} gives baseline competence by stratum.\n\nControls include the unchanged model, an efficacy-selected true-answer \"'$2+2'\"\\\\mapsto4\"'$ sham at five steps, and a post-hoc 20-step true-answer sham at seed 11. Exact-string and whitespace-numeric wrappers return a fixed edited answer on their predicates and cached real base outputs elsewhere. The numeric predicate matches only the entire string consisting of optional whitespace, $2$, optional whitespace, '\"\\\\texttt{+}, optional whitespace, \"'$2$, optional whitespace, '\"\\\\texttt{=}, and optional whitespace. Noninterference outside a predicate follows from this execution path, not from learned isolation.\n\n\\\\subsection{Metrics and statistical analysis}\nStrict normalization accepts standalone numbers and English number words through twenty, without extracting arbitrary numbers from explanations. We retain raw output agreement separately. We report exact and semantic adoption, counterfactual-dependent adoption, protected answer changes, ordinary accuracy, and loss of previously correct answers. Full-vocabulary text KL and NLL compare identical reference prefixes. Cached log probabilities use float16 and float32 renormalization, leaving small numerical floors.\n\nThe predeclared paired contrasts compare KL10 against \\\\ft on protected changes and breadth against KL10 on semantic adoption. Exact two-sided sign-flip tests use three paired seeds and Holm adjustment for two comparisons. Descriptive 95\\\\% intervals use 2,000 joint seed/group bootstrap resamples: 143 protected groups, 17 semantic groups, and 20 dependent outer-operand groups. Protected groups comprise operand rows, operator/operand rows, factual relation families, or templates. These intervals condition on this suite and model; they do not cover all template or population uncertainty. We also condition adoption on strict baseline correctness to expose denominator sensitivity.\n''')\nPY\npython /tmp/write_paper.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
